<div><label for="e-layout">Layout</label><select id="e-layout">
<option value="3" selected>Alcove &mdash; 3 walls</option><option value="2">Corner &mdash; 2 walls</option>
<option value="3">Tub surround &mdash; 3 walls</option></select></div>
</div>
<div class="t-in c3">
<div><label for="e-mat">Material</label><select id="e-mat">
<option value="9">Fiberglass</option><option value="13">PVC / SPC</option>
<option value="22" selected>Acrylic</option><option value="26">Waterproof laminate</option>
<option value="31">Cultured marble</option><option value="42">Solid surface</option></select></div>
<div><label for="e-region">Labour market</label><select id="e-region">
<option value="0.82">Low cost of living</option><option value="1" selected>National average</option>
<option value="1.18">Higher cost metro</option><option value="1.4">High cost coastal metro</option></select></div>
<div><label for="e-demo">Existing surface</label><select id="e-demo">
<option value="0" selected>Sound tile &mdash; bond over it</option>
<option value="1">Tile or unit coming out</option>
<option value="0">Bare studs already</option></select></div>
</div>
<dl class="paths">
<div><dt>A &mdash; Full DIY</dt><dd id="e-diy">&mdash;<small>You buy the kit and fit it. Two days of your weekend and the risk is yours.</small></dd></div>
<div class="hi"><dt>B &mdash; Kit + independent installer</dt><dd id="e-ind">&mdash;<small>You buy the kit, a local fitter installs it. Usually the best value if you can manage the coordination.</small></dd></div>
<div><dt>C &mdash; Full-service, supplied &amp; installed</dt><dd id="e-full">&mdash;<small>One contract, one warranty, one company responsible. You pay for that and for the sales process that reached you.</small></dd></div>
</dl>
<div class="verdict ok" id="e-verdict"></div>
<p class="t-note">Wall area: <span id="e-area" class="mono">&mdash;</span>. Path C reflects observed market quoting, not a markup we invented &mdash; homeowners routinely report $12,000&ndash;$25,000 for a standard alcove. Estimates for planning only. <a href="#quote">Send your measurements</a> for a firm number.</p>
</div>
<p>A note on Path C in that estimator. We are not accusing anyone of anything by including it &mdash; a full-service remodeler carries real costs that a kit supplier does not, and the single-warranty structure has genuine value for people who cannot or do not want to coordinate trades. The point of showing all three side by side is simply that you should know the spread exists before someone quotes you the top of it.</p>

<h2 id="material">What the panels themselves cost</h2>
<p>Panel pricing is usually discussed per square foot of wall covered. A standard 60&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;32 alcove with 80-inch panels is about 69 square feet. A tub surround at 60 inches high is closer to 45. A large walk-in can push past 110.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap"><table>
<caption>Panel material cost, kit only, per square foot of wall</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Material</th><th class="num">Typical range</th><th class="num">60&times;32 alcove kit</th><th>What you get for it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Fiberglass</strong></td><td class="num">$6&ndash;$12</td><td class="num">$550&ndash;$1,000</td><td>Cheapest and shortest lived. Honest for a rental turn</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>PVC / SPC</strong></td><td class="num">$8&ndash;$16</td><td class="num">$700&ndash;$1,300</td><td>Fully waterproof, fast, and reads as plastic in thin grades</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Acrylic</strong></td><td class="num">$14&ndash;$30</td><td class="num">$1,150&ndash;$2,350</td><td>The volume choice. Thickness is what separates good from bad</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Waterproof laminate</strong></td><td class="num">$18&ndash;$34</td><td class="num">$1,400&ndash;$2,650</td><td>The most convincing wood and dramatic stone patterns</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Cultured marble</strong></td><td class="num">$20&ndash;$38</td><td class="num">$1,550&ndash;$2,950</td><td>Very large seamless sheets. Heavy. Gel coat is the wear layer</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Solid surface</strong></td><td class="num">$28&ndash;$55</td><td class="num">$2,100&ndash;$4,200</td><td>Repairable in place because colour runs through the thickness</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p class="tbl-note">Kit figures include adhesive, trim, and colour-matched silicone. They do not include a pan, a door, or labour. <a href="/shower-wall-panel-materials/">Full material comparison here</a>.</p></div>
<p>The most common budgeting mistake here is comparing the cheapest panel to the most expensive one and concluding the category is a rip-off. The more useful comparison is within a tier: two acrylic kits at similar prices can differ substantially in thickness and edge detailing, and that difference is what determines whether you are happy with it in year eight.</p>

<h2 id="labour">What the labour actually costs</h2>
<p>Installation is where the money mostly is, and where the paths diverge hardest.</p>
<p>An independent installer fitting a wall kit in a standard alcove is typically looking at one to two days of work. Depending on your market and whether demolition is involved, that lands somewhere between roughly $900 and $2,400 for a straightforward job. Regional variation is real and substantial &mdash; the same job in a low cost-of-living market and a high-cost coastal metro can differ by 60% on labour alone.</p>
<p>Demolition is the biggest single swing. Bonding over sound existing tile removes a day of dirty work, a dumpster, disposal fees, and the risk of finding damaged framing. If your existing tile is genuinely sound, that is worth several hundred dollars and a great deal of disruption, and it is the strongest practical argument in favour of panels generally.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><p><strong>What should not be cut:</strong> substrate repair and the waterproofing details at the pan and corners. If a quote is cheap because it assumes the wall behind is fine without anyone having looked, that is not a saving, it is a deferred cost with interest. The honest version of a low quote includes the sentence &ldquo;and if we find soft substrate, here is what that costs.&rdquo;</p></div>

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy of two quotes for the same bathroom</h2>
<p>Here is the same 60&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;32 alcove, same acrylic panel, priced two ways. The line items are illustrative but the structure is what these quotes actually look like when you break them apart.</p>
<div class="anat">
<div>
<header><span>Path B &mdash; kit plus independent fitter</span><b>$3,650</b></header>
<ul>
<li><span>Acrylic wall kit, 69 sq ft</span><span>$1,650</span></li>
<li><span>Adhesive, trim, silicone</span><span>$210</span></li>
<li><span>Installer, 1.5 days</span><span>$1,350</span></li>
<li><span>Substrate prep &amp; misc</span><span>$280</span></li>
<li><span>Disposal</span><span>$160</span></li>
<li><span>Total</span><span>$3,650</span></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div>
<header><span>Path C &mdash; full-service, supplied &amp; installed</span><b>$16,400</b></header>
<ul>
