<p>The three amber lines are the ones that surprise people, and they total about $6,400 &mdash; nearly twice the entire cost of Path B. That is not fraud. Companies that advertise heavily, staff a call centre, run in-home appointments, and pay commission genuinely incur those costs, and they have to come from somewhere.</p>
<p>But it is worth being clear-eyed that <em>you</em> are paying for the advertisement that reached you, the appointment that was booked for you, and the commission of the person who sat at your kitchen table. If that structure gets you a bathroom you would not otherwise have managed to organise, it may be money well spent. If you were always going to be capable of calling a fitter, it is not.</p>

