<p>Two patterns worth noticing. First, the tub surround is consistently the best value per unit of visual improvement in the whole category, because you are covering the exact surface that makes a bathroom look dated while leaving the expensive fixture in place. Second, the jump from 80-inch to 96-inch panels is proportionally small &mdash; a few hundred dollars &mdash; and it removes a strip of painted wall that will otherwise need repainting every few years. That is one of the few upgrades in this category that reliably pays for itself.</p>

<h3>Where you live changes the number</h3>
<p>Panel prices are national; labour is emphatically not. The same 60&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;32 alcove fitted by an independent installer can differ by 50&ndash;60% on the labour line between a low cost-of-living market and an expensive coastal metro, and that difference is large enough to change which path makes sense.</p>
<p>In an expensive labour market, the material upgrade becomes proportionally cheaper &mdash; if you are paying $2,200 in labour regardless, spending an extra $700 to move from mid acrylic to solid surface is a smaller relative decision than it would be somewhere labour costs $1,100. The reverse is also true: in a low-cost market, DIY saves proportionally less, because the labour you are avoiding was not that expensive to begin with.</p>

<h2 id="hidden">Costs people forget to budget</h2>
<p>A wall kit covers walls. Everything else in the shower is a separate line, and these are the ones that turn a $4,000 plan into a $6,500 project:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The pan or base.</strong> If the existing one is stained, cracked, or the wrong height, replacing walls around it is a false economy. Budget $400&ndash;$1,400 depending on material and size.</li>
<li><strong>The door or glass.</strong> A framed sliding door is a few hundred dollars; frameless glass is frequently more than the wall panels cost. This is the line that most often blows a budget.</li>
<li><strong>Valve and trim.</strong> If the panel adds thickness, the existing escutcheon may no longer reach. Sometimes a trim kit solves it, sometimes the valve needs moving forward, which is a plumbing job inside a wall.</li>
<li><strong>Substrate repair.</strong> Unknowable until demolition. Reserve 10&ndash;15% of the project against it, and treat any quote that guarantees no substrate work without inspection as optimistic.</li>
<li><strong>Ventilation.</strong> If the extractor is undersized or vents into an attic, a new shower will not fix the moisture problem in that bathroom. Often a few hundred dollars very well spent.</li>
<li><strong>Disposal.</strong> Old tile is heavy and most municipal collections will not take it.</li>
</ul>

