<p>The specific thing worth checking, and the thing this industry is most criticised for, is the difference between genuine zero-percent instalment credit and <strong>deferred interest</strong>. They are sold with similar language and are completely different products. Under deferred interest, interest accrues quietly from day one; if the balance is not cleared inside the promotional window, all of it becomes payable at once. Ask which one you are being offered, and ask in writing.</p>
<p>Also worth doing: compare against a home equity line or a credit union personal loan before agreeing to contractor-arranged finance, and ask whether a cash price exists. Financing costs the seller money, and that cost is sometimes baked into the quote whether or not you use it.</p>

<h2 id="flags">Red flags in a shower quote</h2>
<div class="flags">
<div><span class="n">1</span><div><b>They will not name the panel</b><p>The single biggest warning sign in this category. Manufacturer, model, and thickness are basic facts about what is going on your wall. Vagueness here means the product is not what you are paying for.</p></div></div>
<div><span class="n">2</span><div><b>The price expires today</b><p>A genuine price does not evaporate at the end of an appointment. Discount-if-you-sign-now is a closing technique, not a pricing structure, and it exists to prevent you getting a second quote.</p></div></div>
<div><span class="n">3</span><div><b>The number is only ever a monthly payment</b><p>You should be told the total price plainly and early. If getting a cash figure takes three requests, that is deliberate.</p></div></div>
<div><span class="n">4</span><div><b>No written scope</b><p>&ldquo;New shower&rdquo; is not a scope. It should say what is removed, what is installed, what happens if the substrate is damaged, and what is excluded.</p></div></div>
<div><span class="n">5</span><div><b>A warranty with no written terms</b><p>&ldquo;Lifetime&rdquo; means very little on its own. Whose lifetime, covering what, including labour or only material, and does it transfer if you sell? Get it on paper.</p></div></div>
<div><span class="n">6</span><div><b>No mention of the substrate</b><p>Anyone quoting a shower without discussing what is behind the current surface is quoting a hope. The condition of the substrate is the largest genuine unknown in the job.</p></div></div>
<div><span class="n">7</span><div><b>Large deposit up front</b><p>Some deposit is normal to secure materials. A majority of the contract value before work starts is not, and it removes most of your leverage if things go wrong.</p></div></div>
</div>

<h2 id="negotiate">What is actually negotiable</h2>
<p>More than most people attempt. In rough order of how much it moves:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Material grade.</strong> Stepping down one tier frequently saves several hundred to a couple of thousand dollars with limited visible difference, particularly between the top of one tier and the bottom of the next.</li>
<li><strong>Scope.</strong> Doing the demolition yourself, keeping a sound existing pan, or handling disposal removes real cost from a quote. Ask what each element is worth separately.</li>
<li><strong>Timing.</strong> Contractors have quiet weeks. If your schedule is flexible, saying so is one of the few genuine levers you hold.</li>
<li><strong>The financing.</strong> Ask for the cash price. It is sometimes lower, and if it is not, that tells you something too.</li>
<li><strong>The panel height.</strong> Dropping from 96 to 84 inches saves material, though we would generally argue against it &mdash; the painted band above a short panel is where steam damage returns.</li>
</ol>
<p>What should not be negotiated down is substrate work, waterproofing detail at the pan and corners, or the quality of the silicone. That is precisely where the money needs to go, and it is a small fraction of any of these numbers.</p>

<h2 id="ten">The ten-year number</h2>
<p>Installed price is one line on a longer bill. A tiled shower carries a recurring grout maintenance cycle &mdash; resealing every twelve to twenty-four months plus a partial regrout somewhere in the first decade &mdash; and a panel shower essentially does not.</p>
<p>We built a full cost-of-ownership model that lets you set your own assumptions and time horizon, including the case where tile wins. It lives on the comparison page: <a href="/shower-wall-panels-vs-tile/#tco">run the ten-year numbers here</a>.</p>
<p>When you have a number you want checked against a real quote, <a href="#quote">send the measurements</a> or call <a href="tel:+18887792284">1-888-779-2284</a>. If someone else&rsquo;s quote is fair, we will tell you that too &mdash; it is a faster conversation than pretending otherwise.</p>
