<section><div class="wrap narrow"><div class="sec-head"><span class="eyebrow">Questions</span><h2>Common questions</h2></div>
<div class="faq"><details><summary>How much does a shower wall panel kit cost in 2026?</summary><div class='ans'><p>For a standard 60 by 32 inch alcove, the panels themselves typically run from a few hundred dollars for basic PVC to roughly two to four thousand for premium solid surface. Add an independent installer and most finished jobs land between about $1,500 and $6,000. Full-service remodelling companies that supply and install as a single package routinely quote $12,000 to $25,000 for the same shower. That is a different commercial product with a different warranty, not a different panel, and understanding which one you are being sold is the most valuable thing you can do before signing.</p></div></details><details><summary>Why are one-day bath remodel quotes so much higher than buying a kit?</summary><div class='ans'><p>Because you are buying a different thing. A full-service quote bundles the panels with demolition, disposal, a licensed crew, project management, a long warranty, financing infrastructure, and the marketing and commission cost of the sales appointment that generated it. Some of that is real value, particularly the single point of accountability if something leaks in year three. Some of it is the cost of acquiring you as a customer, which can run into thousands per closed sale in this industry. The way to tell which is which is to ask for the manufacturer and model, then price that panel independently.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is a shower wall panel kit cheaper than tile?</summary><div class='ans'><p>Usually on total installed cost and almost always on time. A tiled shower is not just tile: it is waterproofing, thinset, grout, and several days of skilled labour, and published figures put installed tile anywhere from roughly three to thirty dollars per square foot. Panels can cost more per square foot than budget ceramic and still produce a cheaper finished job because the labour component is so much smaller. Over ten years the gap widens further, because grout needs resealing every year or two and panels do not.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I install shower wall panels myself and save money?</summary><div class='ans'><p>Many people do, and labour is the largest single line in most quotes, so the saving is substantial. The work is within reach of a confident DIYer: panels cut with ordinary tools and the adhesive is straightforward. What catches people is measurement, because remodel walls are rarely square and a panel cut short is scrap rather than an adjustment. If you go this route, order oversized panels and trim on site, dry-fit everything before adhesive comes out, and budget a second day rather than assuming one.</p></div></details><details><summary>What drives the price of a wall kit up the most?</summary><div class='ans'><p>In rough order: material choice, panel height, whether demolition is involved, and whether you need genuinely custom sizing. Moving from acrylic to solid surface can roughly double the panel cost. Going from 72-inch to 96-inch panels adds material across every wall. Tearing out old tile adds labour, disposal, and the risk of discovering damaged substrate. Custom sizes add both cost and lead time, and oversized stock panels trimmed on site are usually the cheaper fix for an out-of-square opening.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are there hidden costs people forget to budget for?</summary><div class='ans'><p>The usual ones are the shower pan, the door or glass, valve trim, and any plumbing that has to move. A wall kit covers walls only. If the pan is old, replacing walls and keeping a stained pan is a false economy, and the pan-to-panel joint is one of the places installations leak. Also budget for substrate repair: if the wall behind the old surface is soft, that gets fixed before anything new goes on it, and it is the single most common reason a quote changes mid-project.</p></div></details><details><summary>Should I finance a bathroom remodel through the contractor?</summary><div class='ans'><p>Compare it against alternatives before agreeing, because contractor-arranged financing is frequently priced above a home equity line or a credit union personal loan. The specific thing to check is whether a promotional offer is genuine zero percent instalment credit or deferred interest, because they are completely different products sold with similar language. Under deferred interest, if the balance is not cleared within the promotional window, interest that has been quietly accruing from day one becomes payable in a lump. Ask which one it is, in writing.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is it worth paying more for a better panel material?</summary><div class='ans'><p>It depends on how long you are keeping the shower. For a rental turnover or a house you are selling within two years, PVC or SPC does the job and the money is better spent elsewhere. For a primary bathroom you intend to keep for a decade or more, the step up to thick acrylic or solid surface is usually worth it, mainly because solid surface can be repaired in place and thin panels cannot. The upgrade that most reliably pays for itself is thickness rather than pattern.</p></div></details><details><summary>How much does a tub surround panel kit cost?</summary><div class='ans'><p>Less than a full shower, because you are covering less area and the tub stays where it is. A standard three-wall surround above a 60-inch tub is roughly 45 to 55 square feet of panel depending on the height you choose, against about 69 for a full alcove at 80 inches. Expect the kit alone to run from a few hundred dollars in PVC to a couple of thousand in solid surface, with installation adding roughly the same again if you are not doing it yourself.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do wall panel prices change seasonally?</summary><div class='ans'><p>Materials do not move much, but installer availability does. Bathroom remodels cluster in the shoulder seasons and around the run-up to holidays, so labour quotes tend to be softer in mid-winter and mid-summer when contractors have gaps. If your timing is flexible, asking in a quiet month is one of the few genuine levers you have. Lead times on stock sizes and common finishes are also shorter when demand is lower.</p></div></details><details><summary>What is actually negotiable in a shower quote?</summary><div class='ans'><p>More than most people try. Material grade is negotiable, and stepping down one tier often saves meaningfully with little visible difference. Scope is negotiable: doing the demolition yourself, or keeping a sound existing pan, removes real cost. Timing is negotiable if you can be flexible about the schedule. What is rarely negotiable, and should not be, is the substrate work and the waterproofing details, because that is where the money genuinely needs to go.</p></div></details><details><summary>How do I compare two quotes fairly?</summary><div class='ans'><p>Get both onto the same scope before comparing the numbers, because they are frequently quoting different jobs. Ask each for the panel manufacturer and model, the panel thickness, whether demolition and disposal are included, what happens to the pan, what the warranty covers and whether it includes labour, and what the lead time is. A cheaper quote that excludes demolition and assumes the substrate is fine is not cheaper, it is incomplete. Once the scopes match, the price difference finally means something.</p></div></details></div></div></section><div class="seam"></div>
